[summary:string]
A Howdy Tech Talk where I interviewed José Valim—creator of Elixir, former Ruby on Rails core team member and founder of Dashbit—about how a race condition he couldn't fix in Rails led him to functional programming, Erlang and, eventually, to creating Elixir; what senior engineers still get wrong about concurrency; and his honest take on AI coding agents.

[duration:string]
May 15, 2026

[place_url:string]


[place:string]
Remote

[content:md]
For this edition of **Howdy Tech Talks** I sat down (remotely) with **José Valim**, the creator of [Elixir](https://elixir-lang.org/). Before Elixir, José was a member of the Ruby on Rails core team; today he leads Dashbit, his own consultancy, and remains a key voice in the high-scale, functional programming community. The conversation was published as **Elixir, Unraveled**: [watch the full interview on Howdy's site](https://l.howdylatam.com/l/jose-valim-video-page).

### The bug that became a programming language
In 2010, José was deep inside Ruby on Rails, chasing a race condition he couldn't reproduce and couldn't prove he'd fixed. That single, stubborn bug pushed him toward functional programming, then Erlang, and eventually toward building something new entirely: Elixir. Today Elixir runs the backend of companies operating at massive scale, such as Discord and Pinterest, from real-time platforms to global logistics, on the same BEAM virtual machine that powers WhatsApp's Erlang backend.

> The best way of solving your problem is disappearing the problem.
> — José Valim

### What we talked about
- **The origin story**, from that unfixable Rails bug to shipping a new language.
- **The design decisions behind the BEAM**, the Erlang virtual machine that makes millions of concurrent connections on a single server possible, with the reliability it inherited from its telecom roots.
- **What senior engineers still get wrong about concurrency.**
- **His unfiltered take on AI coding agents** and what building software looks like in the AI era.

